PROBLEM CONTRACT:
{"goal": {"value": "stop tickets bouncing between teams", "status": "stated", "quote": "The goal is to stop tickets bouncing between teams."}, "action": {"value": "place the ticket in the team's queue", "status": "stated", "quote": "The router will place the ticket in that team's queue."}, "unit": {"value": "support ticket", "status": "stated", "quote": "Every row is one support ticket."}, "task": {"value": "multiclass", "status": "inferred", "reason": "assigned_team names one of several teams, so the answer is one class among many"}, "target_column": {"value": "assigned_team", "status": "stated", "quote": "assigned_team is the label we want to predict."}, "label_source": {"value": "column", "status": "inferred", "reason": "the label is a column of tickets.csv"}, "label_coverage": {"value": "complete", "status": "inferred", "reason": "the label is a column of the main table, so every row is labelled"}, "prediction_moment": {"value": "when the ticket is created, before anyone reads it", "status": "stated", "quote": "Routing happens when the ticket is created, before anyone reads it."}, "time_column": {"value": "created_at", "status": "inferred", "reason": "created_at is the only date or time column in tickets.csv"}, "entities": {"value": ["customer_id"], "status": "stated", "quote": "Customers often open many tickets, so customer_id repeats."}, "primary_metric": {"value": "macro F1", "status": "stated", "quote": "We will judge it by macro F1, since small teams matter too."}, "unknown": ["horizon"], "constraints": [{"kind": "latency", "text": "answer within 200 milliseconds", "quote": "Routing must answer within 200 milliseconds."}], "open_questions": []}

DATA FINDINGS:
- F-duplicates (warning, grain): 60 rows of tickets are exact copies of another row. Implication: Drop the copies before splitting, or keep each copy on the same side: a copy on both sides scores the model on rows it trained on. Evidence: {"duplicate_rows": 60, "rows": 3060, "share": 0.0196}
- F-keys (info, grain): tickets has 3060 rows; 1 column(s) identify rows: ticket_id. Implication: A column that identifies rows can only memorise: keep it out of the features. Evidence: {"rows": 3060, "unique_columns": ["ticket_id"], "unique_columns_count": 1}
- F-classes (info, target): 11 classes; the largest is 19.0% of rows, the smallest 0.31%. Implication: Score with macro F1 (every class counts) beside accuracy, and stratify the folds by class. Evidence: {"classes": 11, "largest_share": 0.1895, "smallest_share": 0.0031, "imbalance_ratio": 62.1}
- F-tiny-classes (warning, target): 2 classes have under 1% of rows or 30 rows: legal, security. Implication: Too few rows to learn or to measure: consider merging them into an 'other' class or routing them by rule, keep them in every fold (stratify), and report their recall separately. Evidence: {"tiny_classes": ["legal", "security"], "count": 2, "rows": {"legal": 10, "security": 7}, "tiny_classes_count": 2, "rows_count": 2}
- F-entity-customer_id (warning, entities): Rows repeat for customer_id: 713 values, a median of 3 rows each. Implication: Group the folds by customer_id: with rows of one customer_id on both sides a model can recognise it rather than learn the pattern. Evidence: {"column": "customer_id", "entities": 713, "rows_per_entity_median": 3.0, "rows_per_entity_max": 10, "later_rows_seen_before": 0.8889, "target_from_entity_out_of_fold": 0.0801}
- F-time (info, time): Rows span 90 days by created_at. Implication: Rows have an order in time: validate on later rows than the model trained on (a forward split), and read drift as a reason to retrain. Evidence: {"column": "created_at", "start": "2025-01-06 00:24:57", "end": "2025-04-06 15:01:19", "days": 90, "class_shift_tvd": 0.068, "classes_only_in_last_third": []}
- F-leak-resolved_by (critical, leakage): resolved_by: on its own it predicts the target almost perfectly; its name says it is written after the outcome. Implication: Keep resolved_by out unless it is known at the prediction moment; ask when it is written. Evidence: {"column": "resolved_by", "single_column_power": 1.0}
- F-name-first_response_minutes (info, leakage): first_response_minutes: its name says it is written after the outcome, but it predicts little. Implication: Ask when first_response_minutes is written; keep it only if it is known at the prediction moment. Evidence: {"column": "first_response_minutes", "single_column_power": 0.0941}

